// Cycle of Life: Realistic Fauna - FotSA penguins (Spheniscidae).
//
// Twenty penguins, from the 45 kg emperor to a kilogram-and-a-half little penguin, and including the
// Waitaha penguin - a New Zealand species known only from subfossil bones, gone by about 1500.
//
// The egg entities need no handling: they carry the "egg" tag and the mod's own filter drops them,
// which accounts for the twenty "-raw" keys this mod would otherwise produce.
//
// INHERITED AND GOOD. Weights are per species and mostly accurate. Group sizes are the striking
// part: 22 with a wide variance, which is the mod correctly modelling penguins as COLONIAL. Combined
// with the declared cap of 4 that works out well in this model - a colony grown to MaxHerdSize is
// ~44 birds in the 0.173 km² the founding check covers, which reads as one dense rookery per stretch
// of coast rather than penguins evenly smeared across a continent. So K is left alone here, unusually
// for this pack: it is doing the right thing by accident of being a colonial bird.
//
// TWO WEIGHT CORRECTIONS. The macaroni penguin is the most numerous penguin alive and the mod ships
// it at 3 kg against a real 5.5; the Galapagos penguin is the smallest of its genus and ships at 4 kg
// against a real 2-2.5. Everything else is close enough to leave.
//
// DIET: PARKED. The mod declares a single "fish" food tag and no foodCategories, so penguins are
// currently neither predator nor forager - they have no food ceiling at all, bounded only by K and
// mortality. They are the flagship case for the planned Fish forage group: a penguin is nothing but
// a device for converting fish into more penguins, and with fish mods on the way that pool is about
// to get a real supply side. When that group lands, every species in this file wants
// "foodCategories": ["Fish"] and nothing else changes.
//
// LIFECYCLES are the substance of the file, and penguins are far slower than their numbers suggest.
// The emperor lays ONE egg and the male incubates it on his feet through the Antarctic winter for
// two months without eating. The king penguin runs a fourteen-month cycle, so a pair rears at most
// two chicks in three years. The crested penguins lay two eggs and almost always raise one - the
// first egg is smaller and usually lost, which is genuine brood reduction rather than bad luck. Only
// the little penguin behaves like an ordinary seabird, and it is the one that lives seven years
// instead of twenty.
//
// Adults are hard to kill ashore - what eats penguins is leopard seals and sea lions, in water this
// model does not simulate - so predationVulnerability stays low and the losses sit in juvenile
// mortality instead, where skuas and giant petrels take eggs and chicks in enormous numbers.
//
// No territory entries: a colony IS the unit here, and the group size plus K already keep rookeries
// apart.
//
// VERIFICATION STATUS: keys derived from the mod's own entity JSON, not read off a live roster -
// this mod is not installed on the pack author's server. A wrong key is inert (the entry is ignored
// and the animal keeps its auto-discovered values); `/col species` lists the authoritative keys.
{
  "species": {


    // ===================== THE GREAT PENGUINS (Aptenodytes) =====================
    // emperor penguin - the only bird that breeds through the Antarctic winter, the male incubating a single egg on his feet for two months in the dark without eating
